{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3103","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3103","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3103\",\"parent_revision\":\"d6625d11007633a2eb16ad08e800cc3c016090f7fbf5e3869def8b2e1f936444\",\"root_revision\":\"d6625d11007633a2eb16ad08e800cc3c016090f7fbf5e3869def8b2e1f936444\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item. The deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict) \u2014 not a mandate to ship the feature. Origin: comparison of graph-engineering research against the development-harness architecture, grounded against the #3060 agent over-orchestration incident (a task-worker recursively dispatched sub-teams after receiving orchestrator-voiced prose; ~$100 of unplanned spend). Shared design constraint across all seven exploration items in this set: the model keeps the judgment (scoring, declaring, deciding semantic dependencies) but writes it down ONCE, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists (sam_schema models, DependencyGraph, the implement-feature dispatch loop, work-milestone). No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 that larger direction is explicitly out of scope for these items.\\n\\nWhat/why: Both verification ends already exist \u2014 single code review (dh:code-reviewer) and the four-perspective quality gate (dh:multi-perspective-review with security, performance, quality, and accessibility reviewers). What does not exist is the connective rule deciding which one runs: today the choice is either always-on (four reviewers on trivial changes \u2014 expensive) or per-session judgment (inconsistent). Graph-engineering practice sizes the verification diamond to the node's risk.\\n\\nMitigates: over-spending review capacity on low-risk changes; under-reviewing high-risk changes when judgment defaults to the cheap path; inconsistency in review depth across sessions.\\n\\nBenefit lands in: quality-gate cost proportionality and consistency.\\n\\nProposed exploration: depends on Item 1's complexity/risk score existing. (1) define the mapping \u2014 score below threshold gets single review, above gets the multi-perspective diamond, with security-sensitive paths (auth, secrets, injection surfaces) always escalating to the diamond regardless of score; (2) trial on one plan spanning trivial and complex tasks; (3) compare review spend and findings quality against an all-diamond baseline.\\n\\nRelated: Item 1 (\\\"Explore complexity scoring on SAM tasks to drive model tier, effort, and decomposition\\\") \u2014 hard dependency, this item consumes its score field.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Experiment or design exploration executed as described (prototype on a real plan, measured comparison, or written design verdict)\\n- [ ] Decision recorded with evidence: adopt, adapt, or reject \u2014 with rationale\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore selecting review depth (single review vs multi-perspective) from task complexity score** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: graph-engineering research comparison\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"95c04ade0026e06a24ecc9e708d5ae9a625aae1215de327d5a7e5e3758c612c5\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIpCTA\"}"}